WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:06.190
Test 2 This is the IELTS listening test.

2
00:00:07.610 --> 00:00:13.250
You will hear a number of different recordings and you will have to answer questions on what you hear.

3
00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:21.210
There will be time for you to read the instructions and questions and you will have a chance to check your work.

4
00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:25.250
All the recordings will be played once only.

5
00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:28.330
The test is in four parts.

6
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:35.750
At the end of the test you will be given 10 minutes to transfer your answers to an answer sheet.

7
00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:39.530
Now turn to part 1.

8
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:51.910
Part 1 You will hear a woman who is caring for her elderly mother talking to a friend.

9
00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:57.650
First you have some time to look at questions one to four.

10
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:21.920
Now listen carefully and answer questions one to four.

11
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.320
<v SPEAKER 1> It's really good to see you Tom.

12
00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.140
<v SPEAKER 1> Since I had to give up work I feel I'm losing touch with my friends and colleagues.

13
00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.060
<v SPEAKER 2> We really miss you in the office.

14
00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:36.700
<v SPEAKER 2> We were all so sorry you had to leave.

15
00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.060
<v SPEAKER 2> But you must be relieved to have more time to look after your mother.

16
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:41.340
<v SPEAKER 2> How is she?

17
00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:45.540
<v SPEAKER 1> Well she's very cheerful but she needs a lot of help.

18
00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.420
<v SPEAKER 2> Have you tried to get any support from the local council?

19
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.120
<v SPEAKER 1> No, I didn't know I could.

20
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:54.600
<v SPEAKER 2> Yes, they offer different kinds of practical support.

21
00:01:55.240 --> 00:02:04.140
<v SPEAKER 2> They realise that carers sometimes need time for all the other responsibilities they have, apart from the person they're caring for, and also that they sometimes need a break.

22
00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:05.700
<v SPEAKER 1> Absolutely.

23
00:02:07.039 --> 00:02:09.360
<v SPEAKER 1> Okay, so tell me more.

24
00:02:10.000 --> 00:02:12.220
<v SPEAKER 1> How do I go about getting this support?

25
00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:15.540
<v SPEAKER 2> Well you'd have to have an assessment of your mother's needs.

26
00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:20.060
<v SPEAKER 2> That means someone would come round and talk to you about the situation and what you need.

27
00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.180
<v SPEAKER 2> So for a start they'd want to know the amount of time you spend looking after your mother every day.

28
00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:25.780
<v SPEAKER 1> Okay.

29
00:02:26.660 --> 00:02:30.660
<v SPEAKER 2> Then they'll probably ask you what sorts of tasks you do for your mother during the day.

30
00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.180
<v SPEAKER 2> Things like if she needs help with getting dressed for example.

31
00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:39.880
<v SPEAKER 1> Right, I do help her with that, and also I help her get into the shower in the morning.

32
00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:41.320
<v SPEAKER 2> Yes, that sort of thing.

33
00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:48.640
<v SPEAKER 2> They'll probably ask you if you do the shopping for her and help her at meal times, and whether she can cope using money.

34
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.700
<v SPEAKER 1> Yes, that's becoming a bit of a problem.

35
00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.600
<v SPEAKER 1> She used to be very good at it, but not anymore.

36
00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:05.350
Before you hear the rest of the conversation, you have some time to look at questions 5-10.

37
00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:39.200
Now listen and answer questions 5-10.

38
00:03:42.270 --> 00:03:47.590
<v SPEAKER 2> And be ready to tell them about anything you find particularly difficult about caring for your mother.

39
00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:54.050
<v SPEAKER 1> So recently I've noticed she's started to have quite bad problems with her memory.

40
00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:58.450
<v SPEAKER 1> If I wasn't there, I think she'd forget to eat, for example.

41
00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:02.570
<v SPEAKER 1> And often she doesn't seem quite sure what day it is.

42
00:04:02.990 --> 00:04:04.490
<v SPEAKER 2> Yes, tell them about that.

43
00:04:05.110 --> 00:04:08.270
<v SPEAKER 2> And are there any physical difficulties you have caring for her?

44
00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:10.250
<v SPEAKER 2> Lifting her, for example.

45
00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.930
<v SPEAKER 1> Yes, she's quite heavy, and I'm afraid of hurting my back.

46
00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:18.370
<v SPEAKER 1> I'd be in real trouble if that happened.

47
00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:24.510
<v SPEAKER 2> They can give you advice about that, and also about how to avoid the possibility of your mum having a fall.

48
00:04:25.090 --> 00:04:25.370
<v SPEAKER 1> Great.

49
00:04:26.390 --> 00:04:32.270
<v SPEAKER 1> So once they've done this assessment, if I'm eligible, what happens next?

50
00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:41.830
<v SPEAKER 2> Well, they might support you financially, so they might help you with transport costs, like if you have to get a taxi to take your mother for an appointment, for example.

51
00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.470
<v SPEAKER 1> I usually drive her myself, actually.

52
00:04:45.250 --> 00:04:47.230
<v SPEAKER 1> So, could I claim for the petrol?

53
00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:48.170
<v SPEAKER 2> You could.

54
00:04:48.670 --> 00:04:50.570
<v SPEAKER 2> And you can claim for the insurance, too.

55
00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:51.730
<v SPEAKER 1> Oh, right.

56
00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:57.030
<v SPEAKER 2> And if you need help with the housework, they can arrange for someone to come along once or twice a week.

57
00:04:57.750 --> 00:04:58.830
<v SPEAKER 2> And one other thing.

58
00:04:59.490 --> 00:05:03.050
<v SPEAKER 2> I hope you don't mind me saying this, but it's important you look after yourself.

59
00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:05.830
<v SPEAKER 2> And it seems to me you're under quite a bit of stress.

60
00:05:06.370 --> 00:05:07.350
<v SPEAKER 1> I am, yes.

61
00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:13.030
<v SPEAKER 2> Well, tell the council, because they may be able to give you some advice on how to minimise it.

62
00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:13.890
<v SPEAKER 1> Really?

63
00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:19.750
<v SPEAKER 1> Though, actually, I feel so much better having talked to you.

64
00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:22.970
<v SPEAKER 1> I'll get in touch with the council straight away.

65
00:05:23.950 --> 00:05:25.530
<v SPEAKER 1> Now, shall we go for coffee?

66
00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:31.090
That is the end of part one.

67
00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:35.950
You now have one minute to check your answers to part one.
